   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( g(x) = \\int_{0}^{2 x} e^{t^{2}} \\, dt \\). Find the exact value of \\( g'\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Multiply",
         2,
         "ExponentialE"
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, the derivative of the integral with respect to its upper limit is the integrand evaluated at that limit, \\( e^{4 x^{2}} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Exp",
            [
               "Multiply",
               4,
               [
                  "Power",
                  "x",
                  2
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The upper limit is \\( 2 x \\), so the chain rule multiplies by its derivative: \\( g'(x) = e^{4 x^{2}} \\cdot 2 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            2,
            [
               "Exp",
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  4,
                  [
                     "Power",
                     "x",
                     2
                  ]
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "At \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\) the limit is \\( 1 \\) and its derivative is \\( 2 \\).",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( g'\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = e \\cdot 2 = 2 e \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            2,
            "ExponentialE"
         ]
      }
   ],
